SupraLINUX KWallet PAM

Official upstream source remains unchanged. Preserve epoch 4, pam_kwallet5.so,
common helper, desktop autostart and systemd user service paths. Use the actual
Ubuntu kwallet6 executable path explicitly, as supported by upstream CMake, and
declare kwallet6 at runtime. KWallet SDK and xkbcommon do not participate in the
PAM module build once the executable path is configured. ECM is the sole retained
Frameworks build input; PAM, libgcrypt and socat remain Ubuntu providers.
Ubuntu qt6-base-dev-tools supplies qtpaths for ECM installation path queries;
the PAM module does not link Qt.

Tests run only in disposable QEMU/KVM guests. A synthetic user has a dedicated
private home. libpam reads a private configuration through pam_start_confdir;
the fixture does not modify /etc/pam.d or accept real credential prompts. A
separate PAM fixture supplies synthetic tokens and observes data from the actual
installed module. Tests dispatch authentication, credentials and session hooks,
verify empty/root/unknown/non-graphical/no-prior-auth/already-run behavior, derive
the real salted 56-byte key independently and check its actual pipe handoff.
A private protocol receiver takes the place of the external wallet daemon; the
installed socat helper sends the real PAM environment to its Unix socket. This
certifies the PAM module and its handoff, not a real wallet or login session.
The unchanged Ubuntu SDK binaries and a rebuilt consumer run after upgrade;
persistent salt compatibility and installed package paths are required. Full
wallet, SDDM and Wayland login acceptance remain desktop integration gates.
